 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Logic-level part: threshold lies between 1.5 and 2.3 V (1.9 V typical) at 250 µA. At 18 A, on-resistance is 8.5 mΩ max with 4.5 V of drive and 5.9 mΩ max with 10 V; Figure 7 shows it rising steeply below about 4.5 V. Bringing the gate to 4.5 V takes 14 nC typical, to 10 V 29 nC typical (36 nC max); the Miller plateau in Figure 4 lies at about 3 to 3.5 V, and gate-to-drain charge is 5.4 nC. Gate-source voltage may swing ±20 V. Internal gate resistance is 1.3 Ω typical, 2.6 Ω max."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current ratings",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "With the case at 25 °C the package caps continuous current at 100 A and the die at 103 A; on a 1 in² copper pad in 25 °C air the figure is 17 A (40 °C/W typical to ambient). Figure 12 holds the die limit of 103 A flat up to a 25 °C case, then falls to zero at 150 °C. Pulsed current reaches 267 A for pulses of 100 µs or less at 1 % duty or less."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat and mounting",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Junction-to-case resistance is 1.3 °C/W max. Junction-to-ambient resistance depends on the copper under the drain pad: 50 °C/W max on 1 in² of 2 oz copper, 125 °C/W max on a minimum-size pad. Dissipation is 116 W with the case held at 25 °C, 3.2 W on the 1 in² pad."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature behaviour",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "On-resistance rises with temperature: Figure 8 puts it at about 1.56 times its 25 °C value at 125 °C with 4.5 V on the gate, and Figure 7 gives about 8.4 mΩ at 125 °C against 4.5 mΩ at 25 °C with 20 V drive. Threshold falls from about 2.3 V at –55 °C to about 1.1 V at 150 °C (Figure 6)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode and avalanche",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The body diode drops 0.8 V typical, 1 V max, at 18 A. Recovering from 18 A at 300 A/µs against 30 V it holds 68 nC and takes 40 ns. One avalanche pulse of 53 A in 0.1 mH with a 25 Ω gate resistor is rated at 140 mJ; Figure 11 gives peak avalanche current against time in avalanche from 25 °C and 125 °C starting temperatures."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "TI's application note SLPA005 covers circuit board layout that reduces switching ringing."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Built-in ESD protection is limited. Keep the leads shorted together, or store the parts in conductive foam, so static cannot damage the gate."
  }
 ],
